<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kōjō no Tsuki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 荒城の月</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Kōjō no Tsuki&quot; (&quot;The Moon over the Ruined Castle&quot;) is a Japanese song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a>. The pianist and composer Rentarō Taki wrote the music in 1901 as a song for music lessons, to be sung without accompaniment, and it was printed in a songbook for junior high school pupils. The words are a poem by Bansui Doi.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Rentarō Taki's music was inspired by the ruins of Oka Castle, while Bansui Doi drew his words from the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai-castle">Aoba Castle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsuruga-castle">Aizuwakamatsu Castle</a>. Taki wrote the song in B minor. A later version by Kosaku Yamada, slower, nostalgic in mood and set in D minor, is also popular as a song with accompaniment. Taki's original uses an E sharp in the second bar, but the modern version usually has an E, probably because the original did not fit traditional Japanese music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The tenor Yoshie Fujiwara recorded the song in 1925 and was the first Japanese singer to make it known throughout the world. Thelonious Monk recorded a jazz arrangement under the title Japanese Folk Song on his 1967 album Straight, No Chaser, and that version is heard in the 2016 American film La La Land.</p><p>The German heavy metal band Scorpions sang it live as a power ballad at the Nakano Sun Plaza in Tokyo, released on their 1978 album Tokyo Tapes, in one of the rare renditions that follow Taki's original closely. Yngwie Malmsteen played it as an instrumental on the Alcatrazz tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1984, and the Japanese band Takeshi Terauchi &amp; Blue Jeans recorded an instrumental rock cover on Let's Go Blue Jeans.</p><p>The guitarist Eduardo Falú recorded it as La luna sobre las ruinas del castillo on his 1974 album Palabras para mi guitarra, and the pianist George Winston arranged it for his 2004 album <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a>: A Love Story. The enka singer Kiyoshi Hikawa sang it live in 2008, and Jackie Evancho recorded it in Japanese in 2012 for the Japanese release of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> from the Silver Screen. The 2020 video game Cyberpunk 2077 has the character Hanako Arasaka sing a version on a parade float. In 1998 the asteroid 8957 Koujounotsuki was named after the song.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kōjō no Tsuki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
